issue.body:
I'm playing around with `Http2MultiplexCodec` for the first time and trying to understand the design/API behind it.

`Http2MultiplexCodecBuilder` requires a `ChannelHandler`.  I was hoping that, similar to `ServerBootstrap#childHandler()`, I could offer it a `ChannelInitializer<SocketChannel>`, in which I declare any arbitrary number of inbound/outbound `ChannelHandler`s for my child channel's pipeline.

This doesn't seem to work though. `initChannel` does not even appear to be called.

How can I setup and order my child channel pipelines, and is there any reason why it behaves differently than initializing normal channels?  I had hoped that each child channel could behave and act exactly like a normal, standalone channel -- for that particular stream ID.

For reference, I'm trying to do something like this:

```
channel.pipeline()
        .addLast(Http2MultiplexCodecBuilder.forServer(
                new ChannelInitializer<SocketChannel>() {
                    @Override
                    protected void initChannel(SocketChannel childChannel) throws Exception {
                        childChannel.pipeline()
                                .addLast(new Http2StreamFrameToHttpObjectCodec(true))
                                .addLast(new HttpObjectAggregator(Integer.MAX_VALUE));
                                // add some SimpleChannelInboundHandler<FullHttpRequest>
                        
                    }
                }).build());
```

I've read the documentation for [Http2MultiplexCodec](https://netty.io/4.1/api/io/netty/handler/codec/http2/Http2MultiplexCodec.html), this [seemingly related issue](https://github.com/netty/netty/issues/7178), and I've looked at the [HelloWorld example](https://github.com/netty/netty/tree/4.1/example/src/main/java/io/netty/example/http2/helloworld/multiplex/server) (but that example just initiates the child channel with one simple `ChannelDuplexHandler`), and it's still not entirely clear to me.
